File: LexIdeals.info, Node: lexIdeal, Next: LPP, Prev: isPurePower, Up: Top

lexIdeal -- produce a lexicographic ideal
*****************************************

  * Usage: 
        L=lexIdeal(R,hilb) or L=lexIdeal(Q,hilb) or L=lexIdeal(I)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note polynomial ring: (Macaulay2Doc)PolynomialRing,, 
      * Q, a *note quotient ring: (Macaulay2Doc)QuotientRing,, a polynomial
        ring modulo a homogeneous ideal
      * hilb, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a finite length list of
        positive integers
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, a homogeneous ideal
  * Outputs:
      * L, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, the Artinian lexicographic
        ideal L in R (resp. Q) such that R/L (resp. Q/L) has Hilbert function
        hilb OR the lexicographic ideal (possibly not Artinian) L in R or Q
        with the same Hilbert function as I

Description
===========

When R is a polynomial ring, if hilb is an O-sequence (that is, it satisfies
Macaulay's Theorem), such an L always exists. When Q is a quotient of a
polynomial ring, there may be no lexicographic ideal with a particular Hilbert
function even if it is an O-sequence. lexIdeal returns null if no lexicographic
ideal L corresponding to the Hilbert function hilb exists in R or Q.

When lexIdeal has an ideal as its input, it returns the lexicographic ideal
with the same Hilbert function as its output. If no such ideal exists, which
may happen since Macaulay's Theorem fails in some quotient rings, then lexIdeal
returns null.

The function now works for quotients by arbitrary homogeneous ideals, not just
monomial ideals. We thank David Eisenbud and Jeff Mermin for contributing their
ideas.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R=ZZ/32003[a..c];                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : lexIdeal(R,{1,3,4,3,1})                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|                  2     2   3     3   2 2   5                                |
|o2 = ideal (a*b, a , a*c , b , b*c , b c , c )                               |
|                                                                             |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : lexIdeal ideal(a*b,b*c)                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|                  2                                                          |
|o3 = ideal (a*b, a )                                                         |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : Ideal of R                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : lexIdeal(R,{1,3,7}) --not an O-sequence, so no lex ideal exists         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : Q=R/ideal(a^3,b^3,a*c^2);                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : lexIdeal(Q,{1,3,6,4,2})                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|             2      2   2    2 2   5     4                                   |
|o6 = ideal (a c, a*b , a b, b c , c , b*c )                                  |
|                                                                             |
|o6 : Ideal of Q                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : lexIdeal(Q,{1,3,6,4,4}) --value of 4 in degree 4 is too high in this    |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|ring                                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Note that we use the Gotzmann Persistence Theorem as a stopping criterion, so
one should make sure this holds in the ring in which one is computing.

See also
========

  * *note macaulayRep: macaulayRep, -- the Macaulay representation of an
    integer
  * *note macaulayBound: macaulayBound, -- the bound on the growth of a Hilbert
    function from Macaulay's Theorem
  * *note isHF: isHF, -- is a finite list a Hilbert function of a polynomial
    ring mod a homogeneous ideal
  * *note isLexIdeal: isLexIdeal, -- determine whether an ideal is a
    lexicographic ideal

Ways to use lexIdeal:
=====================

  * "lexIdeal(Ideal)"
  * "lexIdeal(PolynomialRing,List)"
  * "lexIdeal(QuotientRing,List)"

